import "core"


# DISABLED — a compiler gap, kept as the repro of it.
  A list literal of two or more whose elements CARRY A FIELD does not converge when it sits under
  two further lists. It is the second face of @listFieldMultiple.ct@, which is the same failure one
  level up: there the bound on a field does not reach the nested cons, and here it does not reach
  the nested cons's element.
  Found by building the JSON importer, whose value type is exactly this shape — an object of
  members, a member holding an object of members, a member holding an array of numbers. A JSON file
  nested three deep cannot be read as a value until this is fixed, so what @json/json.ct@ can carry
  is bounded by this and by nothing about JSON.
  What works, each checked on its own:
    - The same chain with a ONE-element array — so it is the second cell and not the depth.
    - The same chain with two @Null@s in the array, which carry no field — so it is the FIELD of the
      second element that is not reached, not the element.
    - The two-element array one level shallower (an object holding it directly) — so two levels of
      list are fine and three are not.
    - A two-element list of field-carrying elements at two levels (an object of two members inside
      an object of one) — so it is not the field-carrying element alone either.
  So it needs all three at once: three levels of list, two or more in the innermost, and elements
  which carry a field.
  It is not the class-typed field. @Num@ below is declared as the concrete
  [/Data/Primitive/Float]; declaring it as the [/Data/Primitive/Number] class fails in exactly the
  same way, so the narrowing of a member of a class to one of its arms is not what is at issue.
  The failure is @Reached runConstraints limit with still changing constraints@, and what makes it
  a fixpoint bug rather than a type error is that ACT is already right and REQ is not:
    ACT ... /Arr(/entries=/Data/List/Cons(/head=/Num(/value=/Data/Primitive/Float),
                          /tail=/Data/List/Cons(/head=/Num(/value=/Data/Primitive/Float), ...)))
    REQ ... /Arr(/entries=/Data/List/Cons(/head=/Num(/value=/Data/Primitive/Float),
                          /tail=/Data/List/Cons(/head=/Num(/value=TopType), ...)))
  The FIRST cell's field is narrowed to [Float] and the SECOND cell's stays [TopType], epoch after
  epoch. Every epoch pushes ACT into REQ, REQ comes back with the second cell still open, and the
  two never meet — so the loop reports change until it hits the limit, having had the right answer
  from the first epoch.
class(Json, [Json/Null, Json/Num(value -> /Data/Primitive/Float), Json/Arr(entries -> /Data/List[$T -> Json]), Json/Obj(entries -> /Data/List[$T -> Json/Member])])

data(Json/Member(key -> String, value -> Json))

every(Json, isa= [/Catln/Doc/DShow])
every(Json/Member.., isa= [/Catln/Doc/DShow])

# The failing shape: three levels of list, two field-carrying elements in the innermost.
/Nested= Json/Obj(entries= [Json/Member(key= "author", value= Json/Obj(entries= [Json/Member(key= "years", value= Json/Arr(entries= [Json/Num(value= 2020.0), Json/Num(value= 2026.0)]))]))])

main{io -> IO}= io.exit(val= 0)
